; The command line of the server binary, over the real binary: `--version`,
; `--help`, the `--stdio` a language client passes, an option it does not
; take, and the `check` mode that was already there. One `.t` per behaviour.
;
; Every input is either a `.t` file, which dune adds to the stanza itself, or
; one of the six entries below: the binary, the three files a test reads to
; check what the binary says against where the fact comes from (the server
; version, the Jasmin lock, and the record of the build's route), and the two
; helpers the one session test drives it through. A file a test reads that is
; not listed here would not invalidate its recorded result, so a dependency
; added later belongs in this list too.
;
; The two helpers are the lifecycle suite's own, for the same two reasons:
; frames.sh turns one JSON body per line into protocol frames, and bounded.sh
; kills a server that stops answering instead of letting the suite wait. Both
; are read-only dependencies; the tests here that name no session use
; neither.
;
; The binary is declared even though dune would build it anyway: the
; dependency is what makes a rebuilt server re-run these tests instead of
; leaving a recorded pass from the previous binary in place.

(cram
 (deps
  %{exe:../../../lsp-server/native/main.exe}
  ../../../controller/version.ml
  ../../../vendor/jasmin.lock
  ../../../controller/build_route.ml
  ../lifecycle/frames.sh
  ../lifecycle/bounded.sh))
